%N A401361 Numbers v such that the symmetric representation of sigma(v), SRS(v), consists of only unimodal parts.
%C A401361 The unimodal parts in SRS(v) may have different maximum widths.
%C A401361 All powers of 2 are in this sequence since their width pattern is 1 and all prime powers p^k, k >= 1, since their width pattern is 1 0 1 ... 1 0 1 containing k+1 ones.
%C A401361 For number v = 2^m * q, m >= 0 and q odd, in this sequence a part P of SRS(v) containing the subset D of odd divisors of v is unimodal precisely when max(D) < 2^(m+1) * min(D). Since the 2-dense sublist of divisors of v that is uniquely associated with part P contains D as its subset of odd divisors of v, 2-dense sublists can be used to determine whether part P of SRS(v) is unimodal.
%e A401361 a(15) = 15 has width pattern 1 0 1 2 1 0 1 containing parts with different widths.
%e A401361 Number 18 is the smallest number not in this sequence since its width pattern is 1 2 1 2 1.
%e A401361 a(6) = T(2, 1) = 6 is the smallest term not in the first row of the array below since its width pattern is 1 2 1.
%e A401361 Arranging the terms of the sequence as an array T(n, k) where row n contains all terms v for which SRS(v) has maximum width n shows the increase in size particularly when n is prime.
%e A401361 Computations of 10 X 10 array T(n, k) up through 1453738 = T(7, 10):
